C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\adj_20261016\fable5\fable5_adj_B_asic.md:45:* **F4 -- REFINE; D2 -- REFINE (one substance).**  The row is right and ONE row kills both RANGE_OK drops (measured, oracle-shaped, both legs, both routes).  Two corrections.  (i) **The class is the macro block's FOUR parenthesised operand sites, not two.**  ADDR's `4*((e)>>5)` and BITMASK's `((e) & 31)` lose their parentheses in the same one-token table (`q1s_A_enoparen`, `q1s_B_enoparen`: PASS, product-identical, filed green), and an IN-DOMAIN low-precedence argument shows the loss as a wrong address (`0x5f6984` for 33) and a lost payload bit -- measured.  The argument that makes the RANGE_OK drops a breach -- the macro's contract is over argument EXPRESSIONS, and product-identity on today's define-only call sites is not macro-equivalence -- makes these two a breach by the same token, and the carrier's class-closure amendment (breaches of ONE class on consecutive findings -> close the class in ONE diff, write the cells into the contract) applies.  The build owes: `SWEEP_SAMPLES += ("1 ? 200 : 5", 200), ("1 ? 33 : 0", 33)` (both legs, both routes, no new case block: the refusal sample kills e1/e2 on four sweep cells; the value sample's typedef kills A on both legs) AND two TX raises in TVAL (`1 ? 33 : 0`, `32 | 1`, address and payload checked at e = 33: A on PROD, B on PROD -- BITMASK is pinned only through TRIGGER, as disclosed).  `meas_B/diffs/shape_*.diff` is a working form (the TX tag names are mine).  (ii) **The landing shape is a SAMPLE, not a new case block:** no NAMED-table change, no row-count change; the gate's equality pins move to `9272 9153 yes` on both legs, "54 samples" to 56, TVAL's message to 114 raises.  Clause: for the RANGE_OK drops, (d) -- `(e) >= 0` -> `e >= 0` is a predicate the fix depends on, weakened on the macro's own domain; for the body-site drops, the contract's own CATCHES clause ("a one-event address or payload offset on the raise route") extended to argument shapes; L1 for both.  DISCLOSE the five other removals (`nooutparen` x3, `nocondparen`, `nocast`) as EQUIVALENT for every in-tree use -- both macro results are assigned to `uint64_t` locals and RANGE_OK's result is added -- and name the use that would see them (`SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR(e) * 2`); not rowed.  If OM's view were upheld instead, the disclosure the coordinator proposes would have to cover all four sites.  I do not uphold it: the instrument's whole warrant is that product objects cannot see guard edits; product-identity used to file a guard edit as equivalent is that warrant turned against itself.
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\adj_20261016\fable5\fable5_adj_B_asic.md:62:1. **F4/D2's "FLOOR -- ROW IT (one TRIGGER compile)".**  Right about the row, wrong about the class: it closes two of the four operand-parenthesis cells the ruling's own argument makes breaches, and the other two are GREEN under it (measured: `A_enoparen`, `B_enoparen` PASS under `pinned_sample`) until an in-domain value raise is added.  That is the cell-by-cell repair the class-closure amendment forbids, inside the ruling that invoked the amendment -- the `#499` shape.
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\adj_20261016\fable5\fable5_adj_B_asic.md:107:2. **One class, three passes.**  Adj item 2 placed TRIGGER's body "outside DEPENDS-ON", so the fixture class was never enumerated as a class and was repaired a cell at a time: pass 2 (`PHYSICAL` 2, the aliased handle, the unread serializer cpu), the F2 ruling (the serializer's cpu and len, calls vs writes), the final pass (width, extent).  My ruling cites the class-closure amendment, applied it to TYPE, and did not apply it to FIXTURE -- the `#499` shape I was ruling against.
