C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\fable5_f2.md:13:**(c) Two-machine config — FILE, disclosure corrected in THIS commit.** Your brief misstates the agreement: Astra builds the two-machine row; grok (ruling 4) FILES it and builds only a one-machine `serial_nr(2)` session. Ruling: (i) route real by reading — `gxemul @configfile` (`main.c:127`), each `machine(...)` block → `emul_add_machine` (`emul_parse.c:461`), testmips setup installs fbctrl (`machine_test.c:337`); `-E` cannot (`emul_simple_init` requires `n_machines == 1`). (ii) It is a NEW LAUNCHER, not a row: at `n_machines > 1` `emul_add_machine` calls `console_allow_slaves(1)`, marking every non-main console `USING_XTERM_BUT_NOT_YET_OPEN` (`console.c:947-951`) — the interaction the grok panel seat named as unmeasured, and it is. Your own criterion for in-round rows was "no new oracle". (iii) `M_static` swaps the operand for a static, not a body-read field — outside (d), disclosed under (e); the contract's REASON was false (R2 fixes it), its disposition was not. (iv) `serial_nr == 1` is clause (e): the announce body never reads it (`machine.c:100` registers it read-only; `emul_parse.c:526` lets a config override — grok's mechanism is right, the row is not owed). Conditions: the filed row names the route (`@config`, two testmips blocks, `focus`, the xterm interaction); the contract says "per instance by construction — a struct member — untested across two machines", never "impossible without laundering".
